WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.325
Part C is three minutes on your own
repository.

2
00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:05.900
Minute one is what you built.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.225
Minute two is what you broke, and how you
know.

4
00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:13.125
Minute three is what you fixed, and what
still remains.

5
00:00:13.475 --> 00:00:17.800
The timings are the whole structure.
Nothing is left for you to guess.

6
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.325
There are three ways to hand this in, and
all three are worth the same.

7
00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:25.425
A recording of your screen, with your
voice.

8
00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:29.325
A slide deck with screenshots and recorded
audio.

9
00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:33.575
Or a written walkthrough with screenshots
and no audio at all.

10
00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:37.175
The written option is full credit, not a
fallback.

11
00:00:37.525 --> 00:00:40.125
Choose it for any reason, or for none.

12
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:42.300
Start with what you built.

13
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.125
On your own screen, open your tools file
and show the tool you designed.

14
00:00:47.475 --> 00:00:49.475
Show its schema next to it.

15
00:00:49.825 --> 00:00:52.825
Then say one sentence on what the tool
does.

16
00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:56.450
And one sentence on what it refuses to do.

17
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.150
This video never opens that file, because
your code is yours.

18
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.025
Most people run long here, and then rush
part three.

19
00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:08.350
Minute two is what you broke, and how you
know.

20
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.150
Run the harness once more before you
record, so your numbers are current.

21
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.550
Then list your reports folder. The newest
file is your source.

22
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.425
Print the finding for one attack case.

23
00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:25.575
This is the knowledge base case from Week
5, article KB dash 02.

24
00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:29.800
Staff wrote that article, and it still
carries an instruction.

25
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.300
Read the result off the screen.
Vulnerable, five of five.

26
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:40.100
The three framework lines are labels from
OWASP and MITRE ATLAS.

27
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.100
They are not the weakness class. That is a
separate field.

28
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.047
The last line names the evidence. An

29
00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:50.150
outbox file, addressed outside the
company.

30
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.950
Now print the table row, where the Class
column lives.

31
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.025
It reads indirect prompt injection from
trusted internal content.

32
00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:03.425
Now show the evidence itself, not the
report's summary of it.

33
00:02:03.775 --> 00:02:08.375
There is one file in the outbox, and the
recipient is in the file name.

34
00:02:08.725 --> 00:02:12.000
One line out of that file is the whole
finding.

35
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.675
An address nobody at the company
authorized.

36
00:02:16.025 --> 00:02:20.475
Then say which weakness class it is. The
Class column has the words.

37
00:02:20.825 --> 00:02:23.000
Grade the world, not the prose.

38
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.575
Minute three is what you fixed, and what
remains.

39
00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:30.775
Show your guard on your own screen, then
run the harness again.

40
00:02:31.125 --> 00:02:36.400
Read the controls first. Both pass, so
normal support work still gets through.

41
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.525
Now the same case, in your new report.

42
00:02:39.875 --> 00:02:42.500
It reads blocked, five out of five.

43
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.750
That is not the same as an attack that
failed.

44
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.475
The agent asked every time. Your code
refused every time.

45
00:02:50.825 --> 00:02:54.475
The evidence line says no message left the
company.

46
00:02:54.825 --> 00:02:58.575
Finish by naming one thing your fix still
does not cover.

47
00:02:58.925 --> 00:03:02.525
Do one practice take with a timer before
the real one.

48
00:03:02.875 --> 00:03:07.875
Open every file you need before you press
record, and do not search on camera.

49
00:03:08.225 --> 00:03:11.675
Close any browser tab that is not your own
lab.

50
00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:16.150
A stray tab raises a scope question about
work that was fine.

51
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.925
If you host the file somewhere, check the
link opens for your instructor.

52
00:03:21.275 --> 00:03:24.125
A link nobody can open scores zero.
